Thought for 3s
✅ Here's a clear recap of the account_fund_assignments table logic:

Purpose:
This table creates a default link between an Account (e.g. "Checking Account", "Contributions") and a Fund (e.g. "General Operating Fund", "Building Fund").

It allows the system to automatically suggest / pre-fill the fund when a user selects an account during transaction entry — which is very useful for day-to-day use.

Core Idea:
Some accounts are almost always tied to one specific fund (e.g. "Youth Program Expenses" → usually "General Operating Fund").
Some accounts can be used across multiple funds.
Some assignments should be non-mutable (locked) so the user cannot change the fund.
Table Structure (account_fund_assignments)
- id (PK)
- account_id (FK to accounts)
- fund_id (FK to funds)
- is_default BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE          -- Is this the suggested fund for this account?
- is_non_mutable BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE     -- If TRUE, user cannot change or omit the fund when using this account
- created_at TIMESTAMP
Example Usage:
Account "Cash" → Default Fund "General Operating Fund" (non-mutable = false)
Account "Missions Expense" → Default Fund "Missions Fund" (non-mutable = true)
This is especially helpful for stewardship — it reduces the chance of accidentally putting a restricted fund transaction into the wrong bucket.

